Hi Omar,

Thanks for the clear repro — this is a known issue in Jac 0.10.2. When a node is deleted, the edge entries in the adjacency tables of its neighbors aren't always cleaned up atomically, so `[-->]` traversal can surface stale/duplicate edge references that should have been removed. It's tracked internally but not yet patched in a release.

Workaround you can ship now:
After any node deletion, iterate the affected neighbor's edges and filter out references whose target node no longer exists (or whose edge ID is missing from the global edge set). In practice:

```
// after deleting B, before trusting [-->] results from A
result = A[-->];
filtered = [];
for (e in result) {
    if (e exists && e.$target exists) {
        filtered +<= e;
    }
}
// use `filtered`
```

Alternatively, if feasible in your pipeline, remove edges explicitly before deleting the node (`A.edge-->>B;` etc.) — this avoids leaving stale entries in A's adjacency table.

And yes, please do file a GitHub issue with the full snippet. Having the minimal repro in the tracker will help us prioritize the fix and gives us something to link the PR to. Reference version 0.10.2 in the issue title if you can.

We understand this is production-impacting for you — we'll aim to get a fix into the next patch release. I'll follow up here once the PR is up so you can test against it before it ships.

Thanks for the detailed report and for offering to contribute the issue.

Best,
